! ============================================================
! 01c  Vibrational frequencies and the IR spectrum
! ============================================================
! Optimization followed by a Hessian.  A frequency calculation
! is only meaningful AT a stationary point, which is why the
! two steps are chained with %job opt+freq -- never compute
! frequencies at a guessed geometry.
!
! WHAT TO LOOK AT:
!   * 3N-6 = 3 vibrations, and 6 modes at ~0 labelled TR.
!     Those six are the translations and rotations; they come
!     out near zero as a check on the Hessian, not as physics.
!   * the three modes, in order: bend ~1700, symmetric stretch
!     ~3800, antisymmetric stretch ~3900 cm^-1.
!   * IR intensities: the bend and the antisymmetric stretch
!     are strong, the symmetric stretch is weak.  Intensity
!     follows the DIPOLE DERIVATIVE, not the displacement.
!   * ZPVE, the zero-point energy -- already ~13 kcal/mol for
!     one water molecule.
!
! Harmonic frequencies from HF are systematically ~10 % too
! high (experiment: 1595, 3657, 3756 cm^-1).  Most of that is
! the missing correlation, some is the harmonic approximation
! itself.  Compare with 03c, which uses a functional.
%method  HF
%basis   6-31G(d)
%job     opt+freq
%charge  0
%mult    1

%geo
 O   0.0000000   0.0000000   0.1173000
 H   0.0000000   0.7572000  -0.4692000
 H   0.0000000  -0.7572000  -0.4692000
